# Dashi (our project)

This team is building **Dashi**: a video agent on the rideshare driver's side. It
reviews a driver's dashcam footage after each trip, shows what happened with the clip as
evidence, coaches on how it could have been safer, and gives credit (with evidence to
contest a low rider rating) when the video shows the driver handled a hazard well.

**Before doing any Dashi work, read `dashi/BUILD_BRIEF.md` and `dashi/PLAN.md` in full.** `PLAN.md` decides scope, timing and who owns which file. It is the
specification: discovery, review pipeline, app routes, UI, evaluation, deployment, build
order. `dashi/RUNBOOK.md` is the team's order of work. `dashi/playbook.json`
defines the scenarios and coaching text.

This rule adds to `build-day.mdc`. It does not replace it. All of its rules still apply.

## Always true for this project

- All project code lives under `dashi/`. The app folder `dashi/app/` is flat
  (no subfolders) because it becomes a ConfigMap.
- Use the skills in `.cursor/skills/` for every VSS call, re-ingest and deployment. Where
  the brief and a skill disagree about how an API or the cluster works, the skill wins;
  tell the team about the difference.
- The deliverable is the app on Kubernetes at `/app` on the team host, deployed with
  `deployment/deploy-app-no-registry`. A local run on the VM is only a quick check before
  deploying.
- Do Phase 0 (discovery) of the brief before writing app code. The scenario list comes
  from what the footage contains.
- Before any re-ingest, save the current captions to `dashi/captions_before.json`.
  Re-ingest one chunk first. Only one teammate re-ingests.
- There is no telemetry and no real rider-rating data. Never claim speed or braking
  force. Rider ratings, comments and routes are synthetic and must be labelled
  "simulated" in the UI and README.
- Coaching text comes from `playbook.json`. Do not invent driving advice.
- The pod's `requirements.txt` is `fastapi`, `uvicorn`, `requests`, `openai` only. Weave
  runs on the VM (`analyze.py`, `eval/`), never in the pod.
- App JSON routes live under `/ds/`. `/api/v1/` on the team host is the VSS backend.
- Never print, log or commit secrets or anything from `/config`.

## How the project is judged

Idea (compelling use of the video search and summary stack), Technical Implementation
(high quality, reproducible, documented inputs, outputs and evaluation steps), Design
(intuitive user experience), Impact (effective use of at least 3 sponsor tools),
Presentation (a 3-minute demo). The full wording and what answers each criterion are in
the "How this will be judged" section of the brief. Weigh design choices against them.

## When the team asks "what next?"

Check which tier of section 11 of the brief is complete and propose the next unfinished
item. Do not start tier 2 or 3 work while a tier 1 check is failing.
